WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.240
Now we've gone through all the different sections of the interface,

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.799
we'll be ready to do some actual coding

3
00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:10.880
and see about creating and connecting up some nodes.

4
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.199
Now here we are in the event graph,

5
00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:16.239
in the example actor that we've made.

6
00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:20.320
Now, we have three different red nodes.

7
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.359
Each of these represent an event.

8
00:00:23.359 --> 00:00:28.190
What they are is a starting point for when you're going to run some code,

9
00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.910
which would be triggered by the engine,

10
00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.020
depending on what they are connected to.

11
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.340
So for here we have the event begin play,

12
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:40.640
the event actor begin overlap, and the event tick.

13
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.399
Now let's work with the event begin play first.

14
00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:50.500
If I was to drag off and add in a print string afterwards,

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.060
this is a simple node which is going to print the word

16
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.679
hello when it is fired.

17
00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:02.740
Now if I compile this, jump back into the engine,

18
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.700
I'm going to make sure that we have show stats ticked here,

19
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.090
just so that we can make sure that we can see the messages in the viewport.

20
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.299
Now if I push play, you can see that we have

21
00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:18.400
hello in the top left hand corner here.

22
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.760
Now that's because this script will,

23
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.159
anything that's connected to this script,

24
00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:29.840
will trigger when the actor is either spawned into the world,

25
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.109
or when the the program starts to begin play.

26
00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:42.290
Now I'm going to drag this down and connect it to tick.

27
00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.530
I'm going to press compile and jump into the engine again.

28
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.450
Now when I push play, we'll see that we have a long

29
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.989
string of hellos down the left hand side.

30
00:01:54.989 --> 00:02:01.870
Now that's because this tick event will trigger when every frame

31
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.069
of the game. So if you're running at 60 frames per second,

32
00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:09.180
that is going to run 60 times per second.

33
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.460
Now we have one other one that we are going to check out here,

34
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:21.419
and that is the construction script. Now if I was to do the same thing here,

35
00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:29.139
and compile, you will now see if I grab our actor and move it in the world,

36
00:02:31.729 --> 00:02:35.659
move it in the world,

37
00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:40.349
so these go away, you can see that this will trigger on

38
00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:49.169
all of the movements. So this is great for if you wanted to

39
00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:53.490
randomize an asset when it was placed in, or you wanted to do some kind of

40
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.250
computation looking at the world when it is

41
00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:03.009
placed into the world. That means that the load from the script is

42
00:03:03.009 --> 00:03:06.530
done at construction time, and saved to the actor,

43
00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:12.110
rather than at runtime, when a lot of other things might be firing.

44
00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.870
So again what we have here is we have the event begin play. This will be

45
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:22.539
triggered at one time when it first starts off. We have the

46
00:03:22.539 --> 00:03:25.659
event begin overlap, but we're going to ignore that. We're going to do this in a

47
00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:30.259
different way slightly later on. And we have event tick. Now this

48
00:03:30.259 --> 00:03:34.740
triggers on every single frame of the game

49
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.139
by default, so be very very careful what you place on this.

50
00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:44.259
You can actually control it by coming into the class defaults and changing

51
00:03:44.259 --> 00:03:48.419
the settings for the tick here. You can either

52
00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:54.770
turn it off, or reduce the speed of it. And then

53
00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.530
finally we have construction script. Construction script will fire on the

54
00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:07.139
point of construction. So now let's go over

55
00:04:07.139 --> 00:04:13.310
the different ways that you can add nodes to the event graph.

56
00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.750
What I'd like to do is go over the three different,

57
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.870
most common ways I think that you'll come across

58
00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:27.540
here in order of least used to most. Now the first way I'd like to

59
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:32.740
go over is something called the palette. Now if we go up to windows and

60
00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.300
select palette in the drop down, this won't be turned on by default,

61
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.980
you'll see that we'll get a window on the right hand side.

62
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:45.060
And this is going to have two sections. At the top this is going to have your

63
00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.300
favorite nodes, and below this

64
00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.819
it's going to have a searchable library of all of the different nodes

65
00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:57.300
that you can come across. Now in order to bring these in all

66
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.389
you need to do is select one, hold down the left hand mouse

67
00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:06.319
button, and drag it onto the event graph. You can see here I've

68
00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:10.819
just pulled a branch over. And again I'm going to remove this by

69
00:05:10.819 --> 00:05:16.899
pushing delete. Now this is quite useful and it's good

70
00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:19.379
if you want to search through everything.

71
00:05:19.379 --> 00:05:26.540
However, it tends to be a little slow when we're programming.

72
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:30.300
The second way to get nodes into the chart

73
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:36.139
is via the hotkeys. Now there are a few standard hotkeys which

74
00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:40.300
will come default with the engine when it's

75
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.500
installed. You can actually go in and update and

76
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.720
change them yourself, but the default ones are...

77
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:52.480
Now if you hold down a B you'll get a branch,

78
00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.680
if you hold down a D you'll get a delay,

79
00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.720
if you hold down S you'll get a sequence,

80
00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.879
if you hold down G you'll get a gate, if you hold down an N you'll get a do

81
00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:11.279
N times, and if you hold down O you will get a do once, and for M

82
00:06:11.279 --> 00:06:15.519
you'll get a multi-gate. Now I'll expect you need to know what all of

83
00:06:15.519 --> 00:06:18.399
these would do, but the important thing to understand

84
00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:23.949
with these is they're mostly flow control. So these are mostly

85
00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:28.959
going to allow you to control the method of execution.

86
00:06:28.959 --> 00:06:33.120
Now the most common flow control node you're going to come across

87
00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:39.360
is going to be the branch node, and now if we look at a branch node you'll

88
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.720
see on the left hand side of this we have a red pin. This denotes

89
00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:51.470
a condition that is a boolean condition. Now we'll go over variables in a

90
00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:56.350
second, but a boolean variable is a

91
00:06:56.350 --> 00:07:03.310
zero or a one, and this would denote true or false usually, with zero being

92
00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:07.180
false and one being true. And now you can see

93
00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:11.980
with this flow control we would have an execution pin coming in from the left

94
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:16.819
here, and then if the condition was set

95
00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:20.019
to true this would trigger anything that was

96
00:07:20.019 --> 00:07:24.899
connected to the true pin. If the condition was set to false

97
00:07:24.899 --> 00:07:29.170
this would trigger whatever was connected to the false pin.

98
00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:32.449
So this is the equivalent of an if statement,

99
00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:35.810
if you would be working within other programming

100
00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:40.189
languages. Our final way of generating

101
00:07:40.189 --> 00:07:44.589
nodes is going to be by right-hand clicking in the background.

102
00:07:44.589 --> 00:07:48.110
And now what you'll see is we have a box

103
00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:54.850
that appears, a search box. Now if I was to search for example

104
00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:59.360
branch you'll see that it will bring up branch.

105
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.600
Now it's also quite smart as well, so if I was to search for if

106
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.560
it will also bring up branch as a selected, and just because it knows that

107
00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.639
if you're working in coding, if you type in if, you probably want a branch

108
00:08:12.639 --> 00:08:15.759
statement. And at this point if I was to just

109
00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:20.560
press enter it's going to create it at the point

110
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.050
where the point where I clicked on the screen.

111
00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.699
Now this is even more useful than other methods

112
00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:32.139
because if you look in the top right hand corner here

113
00:08:32.139 --> 00:08:36.610
we have a tick box that's called context sensitive.

114
00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:41.090
Now what this means is this is only going to return

115
00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.490
values for nodes which would be compatible

116
00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:50.129
with the context you're in. So at the moment this is only going to return

117
00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:54.480
any of the nodes which could be placed on an event graph.

118
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.570
Now if I untick this it's actually going to reconfigure this

119
00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:04.129
and it's going to return every single node which is part of the

120
00:09:04.129 --> 00:09:07.570
system or that you've created or other part of

121
00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:11.309
plugins. And this can be quite useful if

122
00:09:11.309 --> 00:09:15.149
you're searching for something very very specific you might want to

123
00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:18.690
turn this off and then you can do a more thorough search.

124
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.850
However with context sensitive on this is going to help you find the right

125
00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:29.330
nodes that you want to get hold of. So for example if I was to drag

126
00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:35.279
in a cube so this is a reference this is a variable is a

127
00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:40.429
reference to the cube component that I have here. If I was to drop

128
00:09:40.429 --> 00:09:42.429
this it's going to give me an option to

129
00:09:42.429 --> 00:09:48.480
get or set. Now I'm going to get the cube. Now if I was to

130
00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:56.419
hover my mouse over the input pin the output pin for the cube drag off.

131
00:09:56.419 --> 00:10:03.139
Now you can see here actions taking a static mesh component object reference.

132
00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:06.820
So this is going to limit everything that's reported in here to

133
00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:13.460
only things which would take a component a static mesh component as

134
00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:17.460
an input. So if I come in here and I now type in

135
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.970
destroy you will see I get two options I get

136
00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:27.490
destroy component and is component being destroyed.

137
00:10:27.490 --> 00:10:30.610
So if I was to then select destroy component

138
00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:34.450
you'll see it's going to create a node and it's automatically going to link

139
00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:38.429
this up. Now let's compare that to if I right

140
00:10:38.429 --> 00:10:45.899
hand clicked here and I typed in destroy you'll see I get a lot of

141
00:10:45.899 --> 00:10:52.620
different nodes. I get a destroy actor event destroyed

142
00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:56.299
and here is the destroyed component but it's harder to find the appropriate one

143
00:10:56.299 --> 00:10:59.419
that I'm looking for where previously I would just get a

144
00:10:59.419 --> 00:11:03.200
single one and I know that it would connect up.

145
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.720
So this context sensitive is really really useful

146
00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.279
in order to to limit what you're seeing to only stuff that you can

147
00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:18.659
actually use. So that way you've got

148
00:11:18.659 --> 00:11:23.059
that is the most common method for for getting hold of nodes and adding them

149
00:11:23.059 --> 00:11:27.659
into adding them into the there is also

150
00:11:27.659 --> 00:11:33.179
something to be said for copying and pasting. Now if I wanted to

151
00:11:33.179 --> 00:11:36.620
if I got nodes like this and I wanted to

152
00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:40.860
copy them and all I need to do is use the standard computer

153
00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:44.460
clipboard. So with these highlighted if I press

154
00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:51.360
ctrl c I can then move to another another place press ctrl v

155
00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.559
and you'll see that it's going to create those nodes

156
00:11:54.559 --> 00:11:59.200
just by what is copied there. Now we can also use

157
00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:03.120
our other methods for example if I was to press ctrl f

158
00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.519
x I was to press ctrl x it'll take everything

159
00:12:07.519 --> 00:12:11.840
and again if I press ctrl b I can then paste them anywhere.

160
00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.159
That's because when you copy and paste these they're saved to the clipboard

161
00:12:16.159 --> 00:12:22.960
as a as a text value. Now

162
00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:27.200
one more option that we have here is with it highlighted if I was to press

163
00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.440
ctrl d the duplicate it's going to copy

164
00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.240
whatever I have highlighted at that time. That's just a useful

165
00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:44.320
method of doing things. Now let's talk about connecting these up.

166
00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.879
So as you can see here I have three branches

167
00:12:48.879 --> 00:12:52.480
I'll say I wanted to connect this up to the event begin play

168
00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:59.519
I can drag off of this execution pin and you'll see as I move the cursor

169
00:12:59.519 --> 00:13:03.120
over the input pin on the branch I get a little green

170
00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.200
arrow and that means that if I drop that it's going to allow me

171
00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:14.740
it's going to connect that up. Now what I can do so say for example I

172
00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:19.220
was to do that here now if I wanted if I wanted to

173
00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:23.779
disconnect this and connect it up here I can do that by

174
00:13:23.779 --> 00:13:29.220
floating over either of these pins holding down the alt key and left

175
00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:33.470
clicking and let's go to disconnect. Something

176
00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:36.509
else I can do is if I was to fall back over one of

177
00:13:36.509 --> 00:13:41.070
these keys hold down the ctrl key you'll see that

178
00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:46.509
this changes into a small little hat. If I left click I can now

179
00:13:46.509 --> 00:13:54.220
drag that to reconnect to other locations. So with this video

180
00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:57.580
we know how to bring in move around and connect up

181
00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:03.740
nodes. In the next one we're going to start talking about variables.
